---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SeoToolExtras from "../../../components/SeoToolExtras.astro";

const faqs = [
  {
    question: "Why delay subtitles by 500ms?",
    answer:
      "Dubbed tracks often lag picture lock; +500 ms (0.5 s) is a common starting offset when every line is late by a similar amount.",
  },
  {
    question: "Is 500ms always correct?",
    answer:
      "No. Measure one dialogue line; use 300–800 ms typical range. 500 ms is a midpoint for testing, not a universal fix.",
  },
  {
    question: "Milliseconds vs seconds in SRT shift tools?",
    answer:
      "Either works: 500 ms equals 0.5 s. Use ms for fine lip-sync after you are within ~100 ms.",
  },
  {
    question: "Will a global 500ms shift fix drift?",
    answer:
      "Only if error is constant. If sync worsens over time, fix frame rate or regenerate subs.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I apply +500ms in the browser?",
    answer:
      "Yes—paste or open .srt, enter 500 in ms mode (or 0.5 s), apply to all cues, download.",
  },
];

const steps = [
  "Confirm constant delay (same error at start, middle, end).",
  "In SRT shift tool, set +500 ms (adjust after preview).",
  "Download, re-import, spot-check three lines.",
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Delay subtitles 500ms for dubbing — SRT offset guide"
  description="When and how to shift all SRT cues by 500 milliseconds after dubbing. Free browser tool, no upload."
  canonicalPath="/guides/delay-subtitles-500ms-dubbing/"
>
  <p class="badge">Guide · ~5 min read</p>
  <h1>Delay subtitles by 500ms after dubbing (starting point)</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    “Move everything half a second later” is a common dubbing fix—but only when timing
    error is uniform. This guide explains when +500 ms helps, when it does not, and how to
    apply it in an SRT file.
  </p>

  <div class="prose-block">
    <h2>When +500 ms is a reasonable first try</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>New dialogue track replaced M&E but subs were timed to old audio</li>
      <li>Every cue looks ~0.4–0.6 s late in preview</li>
      <li>Export is fresh SubRip from the same picture lock</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>When not to use a fixed 500 ms</h2>
    <p>
      Drift over runtime, per-scene different errors, or subs authored against wrong video
      need cue-level edits or re-transcode—not a global offset. See
      <a href="/guides/fix-subtitle-sync-constant-delay/">fix constant subtitle delay</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Apply +500 ms in practice</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Open <a href="/tools/srt-time-shift/">SRT time shift</a>.</li>
      <li>Set unit to <strong>milliseconds</strong>, offset <strong>500</strong> (positive = later).</li>
      <li>Apply, download, test opening + mid + end.</li>
      <li>If still early/late, tweak in 50–100 ms steps—not another arbitrary 500 ms stack.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Fine-tuning after 500 ms</h2>
    <p>
      Lip-sync often lands between 350 ms and 650 ms. After the first pass, adjust by
      ±100 ms. Pair with <a href="/tools/line-length/">line length checks</a> before vertical export.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="cta-row">
    <a class="btn" href="/tools/srt-time-shift/">Shift SRT by 500ms</a>
  </p>

  <SeoToolExtras
    faqs={faqs}
    toolName="Delay subtitles 500ms"
    toolDescription="Guide to apply a +500 ms offset to all SRT cues after dubbing."
    path="/guides/delay-subtitles-500ms-dubbing/"
    steps={steps}
  />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .cta-row { margin: 1.5rem 0 2rem; }
</style>
